In order to test both the plasma supply from the stellar atmosphere and the efficiency of curvature cooling, we employ a 1D charge-separated setup with different values of the magnetospheric currents, following (Timokhin & Arons, 2013). In Fig. 6, we show tests for two different magnetospheric currents: sub-GJ, j_{\\rm mag}=0.6j_{\\rm GJ}, and super-GJ, j_{\\rm mag}=2j_{\\rm GJ}. In the case of the sub-GJ current, the magnetospheric current extracts the charge-separated flow of electrons with density \\approx\\rho_{\\rm GJ}, moving with sub-relativistic velocity (subplots (d) and (f), respectively), which leads to an efficient screening of the electric field (subplot (e) of Fig. 6). In the case of the super-GJ current, the larger electron density is extracted, n\\approx|j_{\\rm mag}|/(ec), which leads to a strong electric field, E_{z}\\approx 4\\pi(j_{\\rm mag}/c-\\rho_{\\rm GJ})z (see subplots (a) and (b) in Fig. 6) and quick electron acceleration. During the initial phases of acceleration, the cooling to curvature radiation is negligible, and the acceleration is determined by the electric field, \\gamma(z)=2\\pi|e\\rho_{\\rm GJ}|z^{2}/m_{e}c^{2} (orange dashed line in Fig. 6c). When the Lorentz factor becomes comparable to \\gamma_{\\rm rad}, the electron enters a radiation-reaction-limited regime when the radiation losses are balanced by an acceleration in the electric field \\gamma=\\gamma_{\\rm rad}(E_{z}(z)/E_{\\rm rc})^{1/4}=\\gamma_{\\rm rad}(z/R_{\\rm rc })^{1/4} (shown as red dashed-dotted line). We also verified that the total energy of the emitted photons is equal to the full radiative cooling losses experienced by the particle.